SELECT 'core-2026-09-v4','nouns-and-plurals','87b630c771d79eac83e3be02f06e226cf9455e201ca4d4e9ebd574542396373d',payload || '{"slug":"nouns-and-plurals","title":"Nouns: singular, plural and irregular forms","level":"A1","order":2,"summary":"Recognise separate things, form their plurals and make nearby words agree with their number.","guidance":"Read the form and meaning together. Study the contrasts, then explain a worked example in your own words before starting a fresh practice session.","learningGoals":["Regular plural endings","Irregular and unchanged plurals","Agreement around the noun","Plural form is not possession"],"rules":[{"title":"Regular plural endings","explanation":"Most countable nouns form a plural with **-s**. After endings such as -s, -sh, -ch and -x, we often add **-es**. After a consonant + y, replace y with **-ies**. After a vowel + y, keep y and add -s. The ending changes the noun’s number, not its ownership.","pattern":"book**s** · box**es** · cit**ies** · key**s**","commonMistakes":["An apostrophe does not normally make a plural: two books, not two book’s."],"examples":["Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual books: “two **books**”.","Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual boxes: “two **boxes**”."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Irregular and unchanged plurals","explanation":"Some nouns change internally: man → men, tooth → teeth. Others have the same singular and plural form, such as sheep and species. Learn each noun with its plural. A word ending in -s is not automatically plural: news usually takes singular agreement.","pattern":"one **child**, two **children** · one **sheep**, two **sheep**","commonMistakes":["Fish is a common plural for individual fish; fishes can refer to different kinds. The practice specifies the ordinary count sense."],"examples":["Choose the verb: “These books **are** easy to recognise.”","Choose the verb: “These boxes **are** easy to recognise.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Agreement around the noun","explanation":"Use **this / that / a / an** with a singular count noun and **these / those** with a plural. A plural noun used as subject normally takes a plural verb. The words around an unchanged noun can reveal its number.","pattern":"**These sheep are** calm. / **This sheep is** calm.","commonMistakes":["This books mixes a singular demonstrative with a plural noun."],"examples":["Point to one item: “**This** book is in the picture.”","Point to one item: “**This** box is in the picture.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Plural form is not possession","explanation":"Distinguish plural -s from possessive ’s and s’. A plural noun says how many; a possessive marks a relationship. In two students’ bags, students is plural and the apostrophe marks possession. With an irregular plural, add ’s: children’s.","pattern":"two **students** · one **student’s** bag · two **students’** bags","commonMistakes":["Some nouns such as furniture are uncountable in their usual sense. Use pieces of furniture, not furnitures."],"examples":["Several books share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “books”. → **books''**","Several boxes share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “boxes”. → **boxes''**"],"skill":"application"}],"tips":[],"source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"chapter":{"introduction":["Recognise separate things, form their plurals and make nearby words agree with their number.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Regular plural endings","explanation":"Most countable nouns form a plural with **-s**. After endings such as -s, -sh, -ch and -x, we often add **-es**. After a consonant + y, replace y with **-ies**. After a vowel + y, keep y and add -s. The ending changes the noun’s number, not its ownership.","pattern":"book**s** · box**es** · cit**ies** · key**s**","commonMistakes":["An apostrophe does not normally make a plural: two books, not two book’s."],"examples":["Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual books: “two **books**”.","Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual boxes: “two **boxes**”."],"skill":"form","id":"nouns-and-plurals-foundation-1","domain":"inflection","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Irregular and unchanged plurals","explanation":"Some nouns change internally: man → men, tooth → teeth. Others have the same singular and plural form, such as sheep and species. Learn each noun with its plural. A word ending in -s is not automatically plural: news usually takes singular agreement.","pattern":"one **child**, two **children** · one **sheep**, two **sheep**","commonMistakes":["Fish is a common plural for individual fish; fishes can refer to different kinds. The practice specifies the ordinary count sense."],"examples":["Choose the verb: “These books **are** easy to recognise.”","Choose the verb: “These boxes **are** easy to recognise.”"],"skill":"meaning","id":"nouns-and-plurals-foundation-2","domain":"inflection","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Agreement around the noun","explanation":"Use **this / that / a / an** with a singular count noun and **these / those** with a plural. A plural noun used as subject normally takes a plural verb. The words around an unchanged noun can reveal its number.","pattern":"**These sheep are** calm. / **This sheep is** calm.","commonMistakes":["This books mixes a singular demonstrative with a plural noun."],"examples":["Point to one item: “**This** book is in the picture.”","Point to one item: “**This** box is in the picture.”"],"skill":"contrast","id":"nouns-and-plurals-foundation-3","domain":"inflection","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Plural form is not possession","explanation":"Distinguish plural -s from possessive ’s and s’. A plural noun says how many; a possessive marks a relationship. In two students’ bags, students is plural and the apostrophe marks possession. With an irregular plural, add ’s: children’s.","pattern":"two **students** · one **student’s** bag · two **students’** bags","commonMistakes":["Some nouns such as furniture are uncountable in their usual sense. Use pieces of furniture, not furnitures."],"examples":["Several books share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “books”. → **books''**","Several boxes share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “boxes”. → **boxes''**"],"skill":"application","id":"nouns-and-plurals-foundation-4","domain":"inflection","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"domain":"noun-phrases","id":"noun-categories","title":"Common, proper, concrete, abstract and collective nouns","explanation":"A common noun names a class; a proper name identifies a particular person, place or institution. Concrete and abstract describe kinds of meaning, while collective describes a group treated as a unit or as members. These categories overlap and do not directly determine countability. A name can also acquire a descriptive or count use.","pattern":"a **city** / **Lisbon** · a **team** / **courage**","examples":["We need **an idea**, but we need **advice**.","There are **two Alexes** in our class."],"commonMistakes":["An abstract noun can be countable: a decision, two hopes. A concrete noun can be uncountable: furniture, water."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"inflection","id":"plural-only-and-singular-looking","title":"Plural-only nouns and misleading endings","explanation":"Some nouns normally occur in plural form, such as scissors and trousers. Others end in s but commonly take singular agreement, such as news and mathematics in their ordinary subject-area use. Some have identical singular and plural forms. Determine the meaning and noun pattern rather than relying on the last letter.","pattern":"The **news is** good. / These **scissors are** sharp.","examples":["**One species is** endangered; **several species are** protected.","**A pair of trousers is** missing."],"commonMistakes":["Statistics can name a field of study or plural numerical facts: Statistics is useful; these statistics are surprising."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"inflection","id":"compound-and-borrowed-plurals","title":"Compound nouns and borrowed plurals","explanation":"The head of a compound normally receives plural marking: mothers-in-law, passers-by. Some borrowed nouns retain a foreign plural, while others have an English plural or allow alternatives. Usage varies by field and formality, so a current dictionary helps with individual words. The rule is a system with lexical exceptions.","pattern":"mother**s**-in-law · analys**is** → analys**es**","examples":["One **criterion**, several **criteria**.","Two **brothers-in-law** came to visit."],"commonMistakes":["Forms such as formulas/formulae and indexes/indices vary by context. Do not label one established alternative universally wrong."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"}],"recap":["book**s** · box**es** · cit**ies** · key**s**","one **child**, two **children** · one **sheep**, two **sheep**","**These sheep are** calm. / **This sheep is** calm.","two **students** · one **student’s** bag · two **students’** bags"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["sentence-structure"],"readingReferenceIds":["bc-nouns","cambridge-comprehensive-scope"]}'::jsonb FROM g